{ Benko take us even deeper into the world of Cat and Mouse against another great endgame player—the legendary Victor Korchnoi! }
1. Nd4 { Benko: “I was willing to trade my Bishop for the Knight because felt that, ultimately, my Knight would prove itself to be more useful than the enemy Bishop.” } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Ga5c4,Ga2c4] } 1... Nc4+ (1... e5 2. Nf3 (2. Nf5+ { [%csl Rg7,Gf5][%cal Rf5g7] }) 2... f6 3. Nd2 g5) (1... f6 { [%csl Ge5] } 2. h3 e5 { [%cal Bd4f5,Gf5d6,Gf5g7] }) 2. Bxc4 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Ga2b4] } 2... Bxc4 { [%csl Bd4,Rc4] } 3. Rc2 (3. f4 g6 { [%cal Ge4e5] } (3... f6 4. Rc2 { [%csl Gc1][%cal Gh1c1] } (4. h3 { [%csl Gc1] } 4... e5 5. Nf5+ Kf7 (5... Kf8 { [%csl Gc8][%cal Gf5d6,Gd6c4,Gd6c8] }) 6. Nd6+ { [%cal Gd6c4,Gd6c8,Gd6f7] }) 4... Ba6 (4... e5 5. Nf5+) 5. Rhc1 Rxc2 6. Rxc2 { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bc2c7] } 6... Kd7 (6... Rd8 7. Rc7+ Rd7 8. Rxd7+ Kxd7 { [%csl Ge6] } 9. e5 fxe5 10. fxe5) 7. e5 { [%csl Be6] } 7... fxe5 8. fxe5 { [%csl Rd6][%cal Bd4f3,Bf3g5,Gg5e4,Ge4d6] } 8... Rc8 (8... Rf8 9. Nf3 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gf3g5] } 9... h6) 9. Rxc8) (3... Ba6 4. e5 (4. Rhd1 f6 { [%cal Gf4f5,Ge6e5] } 5. f5 exf5 6. Nxf5+ (6. exf5 Bc4) 6... Kf8 7. Nd6 Rd8 8. Rc7 (8. b4 b6 9. b5 Bc8 10. Rc7 Bd7 (10... Bg4 11. Rd5 Be6 12. Rd4)) 8... b6) 4... b6 5. Rhd1 Bb7 6. Nb5 Rd8 7. Nd6 (7. Rc7+ Kf8 8. Rxd8+ Rxd8 9. Rxb7 { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gb5d6,Gb7f7] })) 4. Rc2 { [%csl Gc1][%cal Gh1c1] } 4... Ba6 { [%cal Gc2c8,Ga8c8] } 5. Rhc1 Rxc2 6. Rxc2 { [%cal Ge4e5] } 6... Kd7 7. Nf3 { [%cal Gf3e5,Ge5f7] } (7. e5) 7... f6 { [%csl Ge6] } 8. e5 f5 { [%cal Gf3g5,Gg5e6] } 9. Ng5 { [%csl Ge6] } 9... h6 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gc2d2,Gd2d6,Gg5e6,Gd6e6] } 10. Nf7 { [%csl Bh6,Rd6][%cal Rf7d6,Gf7h6] } (10. Rd2+ Ke7 11. Rd6) 10... h5 11. Nd6 (11. Ng5) 11... Rf8 12. b4 b5 { [%cal Rc2c5,Gc5b5] } 13. Rc5 Rb8 14. Kd4 Kd8 (14... Ra8 15. Nxb5 { [%csl Gb5] } 15... Bxb5 16. Rxb5 Kc7 17. Kc5 Rb8 18. Rxb8 Kxb8 { [%cal Gc5d6] } 19. Kd6 { [%cal Rd6e6] } 19... Kb7 20. Kxe6 Kc7 21. Kf7 { [%csl Re8][%cal Re5e6,Re6e7,Re7e8] })) (3. Kf4 f6 { [%csl Gf4][%cal Ge3f4] }) (3. Rc3 e5 { [%csl Ge5] } (3... Ba6 4. Rhc1 Kd7 5. Nf3 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gf3e5] } 5... f6 6. Nd4 e5 7. Nf5 g6) 4. Nf5+ Kf8 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf5d6] } (4... Kd7 { [%csl Gg7] } 5. Nxg7)) 3... Ba6 4. Rhc1 { [%cal Gc8c2] } 4... Rxc2 (4... b6 { [%csl Gd4] } 5. e5 (5. Rc7+ { [%csl Gc7] }) 5... Bb7 { [%cal Gd4b5] } 6. Nb5 Rxc2 7. Rxc2 Rc8) 5. Rxc2 { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gc2c7] } 5... Kd7 6. e5 { Benko: “In such positions, little things make all the difference. Here I’m gaining central space, getting my pawns off the color of black’s bishop, and (by preventing an eventual...e6-e5) making sure the d4-square will be permanently available for use by my Knight or King.” } 6... Rc8 7. Rxc8 Kxc8 { Benko: “The Rooks are traded and, to the uninitiated, the game appears to be headed for a draw. However, the position is actually very pleasant for White because only he can play for a win while Black will be defending for a long time to come. Why is White better? The flexibility of the Knight is one huge factor (the d6-square in particular is calling to it!), and my central space advantage and superior King also combine to make black’s life unpleasant.”
Note how Benko is raving about the superiority of his Knight over black’s Bishop. We’ll delve far deeper in this “Knight worship” in the section The Pleasures of a Knight. } { [%cal Gf2f4] } 8. Nf3 { [%cal Gh7h6] } 8... h6 { [%cal Gf3d2,Gd2b3,Gb3c5,Gc5e4] } 9. Nd2 Kd7 10. Ne4 b6 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Ge4d6,Gf7f6] } 11. f4 Bf1 12. Kd4 Bg2 { [%csl Ge4,Gd6][%cal Ge4d6] } 13. Nd6 f5 { Benko: “White’s position has improved, but Black has everything covered. Now starts a long game of Cat and Mouse: I make little jabs at his weak points and torment him endlessly.” } 14. Nb5 (14. exf6 gxf6 15. Ne4 Ke7) 14... Bf1 { [%csl Ga7][%cal Gb5a7] } 15. Nd6 (15. Nxa7 Kc7 16. a4 Kb7 17. Nb5 Bxb5 18. axb5 $10 { Benko points out that would be drawn because his King would not be able to penetrate into the enemy camp. } 18... Kc7) 15... a5 16. b4 axb4 17. axb4 Bh3 18. Nc4 Kc6 { [%csl Gg7,Gh6] } 19. Nd6 Kd7 20. Nc4 Kc6 21. Ne3 Kb5 22. Kc3 g5 23. Nc4 gxf4 24. gxf4 { [%cal Gc3h3] } 24... Kc6 25. Kd4 h5 26. Nd6 h4 27. Nc8 { [%csl Bh4][%cal Gc8e7,Ge7g6,Bg6h4] } 27... Bg2 28. Ne7+ Kb5 29. Kc3 h3 30. Ng8 Kc6 31. Nf6 Bf3 32. Kd4 Kb5 { [%csl Ge6,Gh3][%cal Gd4c3] } (32... Bg2 33. Nh7 Kb5 34. Kc3 Kc6 35. Ng5 Kd7 36. Kc4 Bf1+ 37. Kd4 Bg2 38. Ke3 b5 39. Kd4 (39. Kf2 Ke7 40. Kg3 Kd7 41. Nxh3 Bxh3 42. Kxh3 { [%cal Gh3g3,Gh2h5,Rh5h6,Rh6h7,Rh7h8] }) 39... Bf1 40. Kc5 Ke7 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Rc5c6,Bc6d6] } 41. Kc6) 33. Nd7 Bd5 (33... Kxb4 34. Nxb6 Kb5 35. Nd7 Kc6 36. Nf8 Bd5 37. Nh7 Bg2 38. Ng5 Kd7 39. Ke3 { when White wins a pawn } { [%cal Re3f2,Rf2g3] }) 34. Kc3 Ba2 { [%cal Gd7f6,Gf6h7,Gh7g5] } 35. Nf8 { [%cal Gf8h7,Gh7g5] } 35... Kc6 { [%csl Gh3][%cal Gg5e6,Gg5h3] } 36. Nh7 Kd5 37. Nf6+ { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gf6d5] } 37... Kc6 38. Kd4 Kb5 39. Kc3 Kc6 40. Nh7 Kd5 { White has “Cat and Moused” his opponent into a near state of coma and now (with Black bleary-eyed and exhausted) decides to carry out his final assault. } 41. Ng5 { [%csl Gg2] } 41... b5 { [%cal Gg5h3,Gg5e6] } 42. Kd3 { Benko: “Suddenly Black is facing some serious problems. White is willing to enter the race by giving up his b-pawn because the passed e-pawn or h-pawn (whichever one White creates) will prove faster. } (42. Nxh3? Ke4 $10) 42... Bc4+ 43. Ke3 Bf1 44. Kf2 Bg2 { [%csl Bf1][%cal Gf2g3,Gg2f1] } 45. Kg3?? { Evidently Korchnoi wasn’t the only one tired from the battle. Benko could have forced a win by 45.Ke2! } (45. Ke2! { allows the King to stop black’s b-pawn in the key variation } 45... Kc4 46. Nxe6 Kxb4 47. Nd4 Kc5 48. Nxf5 b4 49. Kd2 $18) 45... Kc4 46. Nxe6 Kxb4 47. Nd4 Kc4?? { Benko: “Korchnoi finally cracks." } { [%cal Gb5b2] } (47... Kc5! { when the game would be drawn: } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gd4f5] } 48. Nxf5 b4 49. Ne3 (49. e6 { [%cal Gc5c6] } 49... Kc6 50. Nd4+ Kd6 51. f5 Be4 52. f6 Bg6 53. Kxh3 Kd5 54. Nc2 { [%cal Gb4b2] }) 49... b3 50. Nd1 Kc4 51. e6 Bd5 52. e7 Bf7 53. Kxh3 Kd3 54. f5 Kd2 55. Nb2 Kc3 $10 { and a peaceful conclusion can’t be avoided. }) 48. Nxf5 b4 49. Ne3+ Kd3 50. Nxg2 hxg2 51. Kxg2 b3 52. e6 b2 53. e7 b1=Q 54. e8=Q { The rest is easy for White. } 54... Qa2+ 55. Kg3 Qa7 56. Qb5+ Ke4 57. Qe5+ Kd3 58. f5 Qh7 59. h4 Qg8+ 60. Kf4 Qc4+ 61. Kg5 Qg8+ 62. Kh6 Qf8+ 63. Qg7 Qd8 (63... Qxf5 64. Qg6 Ke4 65. h5 $18) 64. f6 { 1-0
Are you getting a feel for the power of Cat and Mouse yet? Remember, the concepts in this chapter are not things to be memorized, they are ideas that need to be embraced.
Since Benko is doing such a great job teaching us about the effectiveness of Cat and Mouse, I’ll let him drive the message home by borrowing one more example from Pal Benko: "My Life, Games, and Compositions" This time he Cat and Mouses one of the greatest endgame players of all time. } *


