{ White, with an extra pawn, a superior King, and a strong Knight vs. a Bishop should win. However, it won’t be easy. This is what Benko had to say: “The game was adjourned here and after analyzing at leisure, I realized the win would take quite a long time. If victory is to be achieved, the white King will have to find a way to penetrate. Unfortunately, black’s King and Bishop makethis difficult. As a result, I decided that it was time for a bit of Cat and Mouse: I would patiently maneuver, hoping to make Smyslov less alert. I knew that such long-term torture from a passive position would be very hard for Black to deal with, so I sat down
and resumed the game ready for a lengthy siege.” } { [%csl Gb4] }
1. f5+ { Benko: “The first order of business is to push back the black King. It isn’t optimalto place my pawn on a vulnerable white square, but I had no choice in the matter.”
I should add that, after 1.f5+, Black is completely passive and can’t do anything but go back and forth and hope that White can’t find a way to finish him off. Such positions virtually scream for Cat and Mouse treatment! } { [%cal Ge6d7] } (1. Nc4 Kf5 { [%cal Ge6f5] }) 1... Kd7 2. Nc4 (2. h4 h5 { [%csl Gd6] } 3. Nc4 Bxc4 (3... Bb5 { [%cal Gb5a4,Ga4d1] } 4. Nd6 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd6e4] } (4. Ne3 Be2 5. Ng2 { [%csl Gh5] } 5... Bd1 6. Nf4 Bf3 { [%cal Gd4c4,Bb4b5] } 7. Kc4 Be4 8. Nxh5 Bxf5 9. Nxf6+ { [%csl Gb8,Gh8] }) 4... Be2 { [%cal Ge2h5] } 5. Ne4 { [%cal Ge4f6] } 5... Ke7 { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gb4b5,Ge2b5] } 6. Ng3 { [%cal Gd4c4] } 6... Bd1 7. b5 (7. Kc3) (7. Kc4 Ba4 { [%cal Ga4b5,Gg3h5] } 8. Nxh5) 7... cxb5 8. Kd5 Bf3+ 9. Kd4) 4. Kxc4 Kc7 5. b5 cxb5+ { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gb5c5] } 6. Kxb5 Kb7 7. c6+ Kc7 8. Kc5 Kc8 9. Kd6 Kd8 { [%cal Bc6c7] } 10. c7+ { [%csl Rc7] } 10... Kc8 11. Ke6 Kxc7 12. Kxf6 Kd7 13. Kg6 Ke7 14. Kxh5 (14. f6+ Kf8 15. Kxh5 Kf7 16. Kg5) 14... Kf6 15. Kh6 Kxf5 16. h5 Kf6) 2... Bb5 (2... Bxc4 3. Kxc4 { [%cal Gb4b5] } 3... Kc7 4. b5 cxb5+ 5. Kxb5 Kb7 6. c6+ Kc7 7. Kc5 Kb8 (7... Kc8 8. Kd6 Kd8 9. c7+ (9. Ke6 Ke8 (9... Kc8 10. Kxf6 Kc7 11. Kg7 Kxc6 { [%csl Gf8][%cal Gf5f8] }) 10. c7 { [%csl Gc8][%cal Gc7c8] }) 9... Kc8 { [%cal Gd6e6,Ge6f6,Gf6g7,Gg7h7] } 10. Ke6 Kxc7 { [%cal Gf6f7] } 11. Kxf6) 8. c7+ { [%cal Rc5d6,Gd6e6,Re6f6] } (8. Kd6 { [%cal Bd6f6,Gf6h7] } 8... Kc8 9. Ke6 Kc7 10. Kxf6 Kxc6 11. Ke7 { [%csl Gf8][%cal Gf5f8] })) 3. Kc3 (3. h4 { [%csl Gh7][%cal Bd4g4,Bg4h5,Gh5h7] }) (3. Ne3 Ba4 4. Ng4 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg4f6] } 4... Ke7 5. Kc4 Bb5+ 6. Kc3 { [%csl Gh7] } (6. Kd4 Be2 7. Ne3 Bb5 8. h4 h5 9. Nc2 Be2 10. Kc3 Bg4 11. Nd4 Kd7 12. Kc4 Bh3 13. b5 Bf1+ 14. Kb4 cxb5 15. Nxb5 Bxb5 16. Kxb5)) (3. Nd6 Ba6 4. Ne4 Ke7) 3... Ba6 { [%csl Gc4,Gg3,Be2,Bf1] } 4. Kb3 (4. Ne3 Be2 5. Nc4) 4... Bb5 (4... Bxc4+ 5. Kxc4 { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gb4b5] } 5... Kc7 6. b5 cxb5+ 7. Kxb5 Kb7 8. c6+ Kc7 9. Kc5 Kc8 10. Kd6 Kd8 11. c7+ Kc8 12. Ke6 Kxc7 13. Kxf6) 5. Nd6 { [%cal Gd6e4,Ge4f6] } 5... Be2 6. Kc3 { Continuing his Cat and Mouse torture, but the more straightforward 6.Ka4 seems to win by force: } { [%cal Ge2c4] } (6. Ka4!? { [%csl Bd6] } 6... Bd3 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gd3f5] } 7. Ka5 { [%csl Gb6] } 7... Kc7 (7... h5 8. Kb6 h4 9. b5 cxb5 10. Nxb5) 8. h4 h6 9. h5 Be2 (9... Kd7 10. Kb6 Bc2 11. Kb7 Bd3 12. Nf7 Bxf5 13. Nxh6 Be6 14. Nf7 Bxf7 15. h6 Bg6 16. b5 $18) 10. Ne8+ Kd8 11. Nxf6 $18) 6... Ke7 7. Nc4 Kd7 8. Ne3 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Ge3g4,Gg4f6] } 8... Ke7 { [%csl Gh7] } 9. Nc2 Kd7 10. Nd4 Bf1 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gc3d2,Gd2e3,Ge3f4,Gf4g4,Gg4h4,Gh6h7] } 11. Kd2 Bc4 12. Ke3 { [%cal Gc4e2] } 12... Bf7 13. Kf4 Bc4 14. Kg4 Bf7 15. Kh4 Kc7 { Smyslov wrongly assumed that, after 15...Kc7, White wouldn’t be able to win the h-pawn because the Knight gets trapped. However, Black was dead lost in any case: } { [%csl Be4][%cal Ge4f6] } (15... Be8 16. b5 cxb5 17. Nxb5 $18 { and the threat of Nd6 or even Nc3-e4, mixed with ideas of pushing the c-pawn and/or advancing the King to h5 and
h6, is overwhelming. }) 16. Ne6+ { [%csl Gc7,Ge6][%cal Ge6c7] } 16... Kd7 { [%cal Ge6f8,Gf8h7] } (16... Kc8 17. Nf8 { [%csl Gh7][%cal Gf8h7] } 17... Bg8 { [%cal Gh4h5,Gh5h6,Gf7h5] } (17... h6 18. Ng6 { [%csl Gh6][%cal Gh4h5,Gh5h6] }) 18. Kh5 $18) (16... Bxe6 17. fxe6 { [%csl Re6] } 17... Kd8 18. Kg4 { [%cal Gg4f5,Gf5f6] } (18. b5 cxb5 19. c6 b4 20. e7+ Kxe7 21. c7 { [%cal Re7d7] }) 18... Ke7 19. Kf5 h5 20. h4 Kf8 { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rf5f6] }) 17. Nf8+! Ke7 18. Nxh7 Bg8 19. Ng5! { [%cal Gf6g5] } 19... fxg5+ 20. Kxg5 { White’s pawns are unstoppable since Black must also take a b4-b5 break into account. } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh2h4,Gh4h5,Gf5f6,Gh5h6,Gh6h7,Gf6f7] } 20... Kf7 21. h4 Kg7 22. f6+ Kf8 23. h5 Bc4 24. Kf4 Kf7 25. Ke5 Bd3 { [%cal Gh5h6] } 26. h6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Ge5d6] } 26... Kg6 { [%csl Gh7][%cal Gh6h7] } 27. Ke6 Bc4+ 28. Ke7 { [%cal Gf6f7] } 28... Bd5 { [%cal Gf6f7] } 29. f7 { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gd5f7,Gh6h7] } 29... Bxf7 { [%cal Gh6h7,Gh7h8] } 30. h7 { 1-0
Obviously, Cat and Mouse and patience go hand in hand. Our next example shows IM Jack Peters (who dominated Southern California chess for almost three decades!) picking his opponent apart in unhurried and impressive fashion. } { [%csl Gb4,Gc5] } 30... Kxh7 { [%csl Rf7][%cal Ge7f7] } *


