{ Black to move. Black fixes white’s pawns on light squares before stepping into the minor piece endgame. This shows how one can prepare a favorable endgame right in the middlegame. }
1... e5! { A strong move that clamps down on d4, prepares ...Nc5-e6-d4, and also fixes white’s e-pawn on the light-colored e4-square. White’s position is now
extremely uncomfortable. } 2. Qd1 (2. Rxd8+ Qxd8) 2... Rxd2 { [%csl Gd2] } 3. Qxd2 Ne6 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge6d4,Ge5d4] } 4. Ne2 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc7e7,Ge7c5] } 4... Qe7 { [%csl Ga4][%cal Gc6c5,Gb7c6,Gc6a4] } 5. Nc1 Kg7 6. Nb3 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gc4c5] } 6... c5 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge5d4,Gc5d4] } 7. Nc1 Bc6 { [%csl Ga4][%cal Gc6a4] } 8. Qd1 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3,Ge7d7] } 8... Qd7 { Simultaneously hitting a4 and d1 and forcing White to enter a very poor minor piece endgame. } { [%csl Gd1][%cal Rc6a4,Yd7d1,Gd7a4] } 9. Qxd7 Bxd7 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge6d4] } 10. b3 { With so many of white’s pawns on light squares, it’s hard to imagine White saving the game. All Black needs is patience—slow improvements in pawn structure, kingside space, and piece position, followed by penetration into white’s camp, should be enough to eventually claim the full point. } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge6d4] } 10... Nd4 { Now white’s Knight is stuck doing guard duty to the b3-pawn. } 11. Kg1 f5 { Adding to black’s list of advantages by grabbing kingside space. } 12. Bd3 (12. exf5 Bxf5) 12... Bc6 { Putting pressure on e4 and letting White worry whether Black will leave the pawn on f5 or push to f4 with even more kingside territory. } 13. exf5 gxf5 { [%cal Gg7f6,Gf6g5,Gg5f4] } 14. Kf2 { [%csl Gf6] } 14... Kf6 { [%csl Gf4,Gh4][%cal Gf5f4,Gh7h5,Gh5h4] } 15. Ke3 { [%cal Gf6g5] } 15... Kg5 16. Bb1 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh7h4] } 16... h5 17. Bd3 h4 { This threatens to undermine the f3-pawn by...h3. } { [%csl Yg2][%cal Rh4h3] } 18. h3? { This is too much! Putting another pawn on a light square can’t be healthy. He should have played 18.Be2 with the understanding that he was going to passively suffer for a long time to come. } 18... Be8! { [%cal Gg5f4,Gf4g3,Gg3g2] } 19. Be2 Bg6 { Preparing ...f4+ followed by a Bishop penetration on the b1-h7 diagonal. } { [%cal Gf5f4] } 20. Bd1 (20. f4+ { also possible and desperate } 20... exf4+ 21. Kf2 $19 { though Black wins comfortably enough. } 21... Nxe2! 22. Nxe2 (22. Kxe2 Bh5+ 23. Kf2 Bd1 24. Ke1 Bc2 $19) 22... Bh5 23. Ng1 (23. Nc3 Kf6 24. Nd5+ Ke5 25. Nxb6 Bd1 $19) 23... Bd1 24. Nf3+ Kf6 25. Nd2 Ke5 26. Ke1 Bc2 27. Nf3+ Kf6 28. Nd2 f3! 29. gxf3 Ke5 $19 { and it’s time for White to shake hands. }) 20... f4+ 21. Kf2 { [%cal Gg6b1,Gb1a2,Ga2b3] } 21... Bb1 (21... Bc2) 22. Ne2 (22. Ke1 $19 { but even the more resilient, couldn’t hope to nullify the accumulated Black advantages Both 22...e4 and 22...Bf5 23.Na2 Nc2+ would ultimately drag White down to defeat. }) 22... e4 23. fxe4 { [%cal Gd4e2] } (23. Nxd4 e3+ $19 { horrific. } { [%cal Gc5d4] }) 23... Nxe2! 24. Bxe2 Bxe4 { The game is over. White can’t stop ...Bce2 (winning material) and/or .. .Kf6- e5-d4. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gg5f5,Gf5e5,Ge5d4,Gd4c3,Gc3b3] } (24... Bc2) 25. Bg4 (25. Bd1 Kf6 $19 { also easy for Black. } (25... Kf5 26. Bg4+ Ke5 27. Bd1 Bc6)) 25... Bc2 26. Kf3 { [%csl Ba1][%cal Bc2b3,Bb3c4,Rb3a4,Ba5a1] } (26. Bd7 Bxb3 27. Bb5) 26... Bd1+ 27. Ke4 Bxg4 28. hxg4 Kxg4 $19 { 0-1
I made Cat and Mouse the first topic in Part Eight since the idea of patience will overflow into many kinds of endgames. } { [%csl Gh1][%cal Gg4g3,Gg3g2,Gh4h1] } *


