[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ In the middlegame, it’s quite common to see a player saddling his opponent with as many weaknesses as possible. In general, it’s hard to tear down a defensive position if there is only one weakness to attack. However, two or more weaknesses (these can be weak squares, weak pawns, material disadvantage—anything that causes a problem for the opponent) often spread the defense enough to allow breakthroughs to occur.

Here’s a nice middlegame example that spills over into the endgame.

White is clearly better thanks to his passed pawn (which can push to d6 at will) and the loosening of black’s King position. However, the passed pawn
alone won’t win the game—another weakness will be needed to stretch black’s defense to the breaking point. }
1. a4! { Intending a5 followed by axb6, weakening the b6-pawn and the b5-square. In some lines the a-pawn might also march on to a6 where, with NbS to follow,
it turns a7 into a target. } 1... a5?! { Too panicky! Now White has a strong passed pawn to push, a weak b6-pawn to attack, and a juicy b5-square to live on. More than enough to force a win. } { [%cal Ge2b5,Rg7c7] } (1... Rfe8 2. a5 bxa5 (2... Qc7 3. axb6 axb6 4. Nb5 Ba6 { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gb5c7] } 5. Nxc7 Bxe2 6. Rxa8 { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ga8g8] } 6... Rxa8 7. Nxa8 Bxd1 { [%csl Gd8][%cal Gd5d8] } 8. d6 Kf8 9. Nc7 { [%csl Ge8][%cal Gc7e8,Gd6e7] } 9... Kf7) (2... Kh8 3. axb6 axb6 4. Qb5 { [%csl Rb6][%cal Rb5b6] }) 3. Qb5 { [%cal Rb5a5,Gb5c5] }) (1... a6 2. a5 b5 { [%csl Ga6,Gb5,Gc4][%cal Gc5c4] }) (1... Kh8 2. a5 { [%csl Ga5] } 2... Kg8 (2... bxa5) 3. axb6 axb6 { [%csl Bb5] }) 2. Qb5 { [%csl Gb6] } 2... Qc7 { [%csl Gd5,Gb6][%cal Gd1d6,Gd6b6] } 3. d6! Qc6 { allows us to see how an “innocent” weakening of the b6-pawn can have profound repercussions on a defender’s position. } { [%csl Gg2][%cal Gc6g2] } (3... Qd8? { In the actual game, and lost with hardly a whimper after } 4. Nd5 Bxd5 5. Rxd5 e4 6. Rad1 Kg7 7. d7 Rf6 8. h3 Re6 9. Qc4 Re7 10. Rd6 Ra7 11. Qc3+ Kg8 12. Qf6 Ra8 13. Qxe7 { 1-0 } 13... Qxe7 14. d8=Q+ Rxd8 15. Rxd8+ Kg7 16. R1d7 Qxd7 17. Rxd7+ $18) 4. Qxc6 Bxc6 5. d7! Rfd8 6. Rd6 { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd6b6,Gc6d7] } 6... Bxd7 (6... Rxd7) 7. Rad1 { Thepin is killing Black } { [%csl Gd7] } 7... Ra7 8. Nd5 { The weakness of b6 has become extremely important since White threatens both Nf6+ (which can be defended by ...Kf7) and Nxb6 (which can’t be stopped). } 8... Kf7 9. Nxb6 { and now after } { [%cal Gd6d7,Gd1d7,Gb6d7] } 9... Ke7 { White can torture his helpless (and quite lost) opponent in several ways } 10. f3 { Clearly, the creation of a second weakness/target in the enemy camp is a far from trivial accomplishment! } (10. b3) (10. R6d5 { all leap to mind }) (10. Rxd7+ Rdxd7 11. Rxd7+ Rxd7 12. Nxd7) (10. Nc4 { [%csl Ge5] } 10... e4 11. Ne5 Rc7 12. Rxd7+ Rcxd7 13. Nc6+ Ke8) 10... Rb7 11. Rxd7+ { [%csl Gd7] } (11. Kf2) 11... Rdxd7 12. Rxd7+ Rxd7 13. Nxd7 Kxd7 *


