[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): The principe of Two Weaknesses (Rubinstein - C"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/3e4UHyHF"]
[FEN "8/6pp/p1r1k3/Pp2p3/4K3/1PPR4/6PP/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ An untrained eye might consider things to be fairly balanced. However, White enjoys certain advantages that decisively tip the scales in his favor. Here’s the shortlist:

- Superior King position.
- A passed c-pawn after a well timed c3-c4 push.
- Black’s a6 pawn is a target that can be attacked if white’s Rook is able to penetrate into the enemy position.

Two problems/questions must be addressed. One is whether black’s e-pawn is a strength or weakness. The other is finding a way for White to infiltrate
with his Rook. }
1. Rh3! { ‘This maneuver aims to turn black’s kingside pawns into weaknesses. Defending the h- and g-pawns will force Black to make concessions which, when taking white’s other plusses into account, should prove decisive (after all, there’s only so much “bad news” a position can handle!). } (1. Rg3 { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gg7g6] } 1... Rc7) 1... h6 { [%csl Gh3,Bg6][%cal Gg6c6] } (1... g6 { White can successfully deal with that move. } 2. Rxh7 Rxc3 3. Ra7 Rc6 (3... Rxb3 4. Rxa6+ Kf7 5. Rb6 $18) 4. Rg7 Kf6 5. Rb7 Rc3 6. Rb6+ Kg5 7. Rxa6 Rxb3 8. Re6 b4 9. Rxe5+ Kg4 10. Re6 g5 11. a6 Ra3 12. Kd4 Ra2 13. Kc4 Rxg2 14. a7 Ra2 15. Re7 Kh3 16. Kxb4 Kxh2 17. Kb3 Ra6 18. Re2+ Kg3 19. Ra2 Rxa7 20. Rxa7 Kf3 21. Rf7+ Ke3 22. Rg7 Kf4 23. Kc2 $18 { stopping black’s pawn and winning the game. }) (1... Rc8 2. Rxh7 Rxc3 3. Rxg7 Rxb3 4. Rg6+ { [%cal Gg6a6] } 4... Kd7 5. Rxa6) 2. Rg3 { The two new weaknesses on g6 and g7 have placed Black under serious pressure to which there is no completely adequate response. } 2... Kf6 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Be4d5] } (2... g5 { [%csl Gh6] } 3. Rh3 $18 { a move low in subtlety but high in strength. } { [%csl Rh6] } (3. h4 { [%cal Gg5g4] } 3... g4 4. Rxg4 Rxc3 5. Rg6+ Kf7 { [%csl Ga6][%cal Gg6a6] } 6. Rxa6 Rxb3 7. Kxe5) 3... h5 { [%cal Gc6c3] } (3... Kf7 { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] }) 4. Rxh5 { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gc3b3,Gh2h6] } 4... Rxc3 5. Rh6+ { [%csl Gg5] } 5... Kd7 6. Rxa6 { [%csl Ga6] } 6... Rxb3 7. Kxe5) (2... Rc7 { [%csl Ba6][%cal Gg3g6,Gg6a6] }) 3. Kd5 { White’s vast superiority is now strikingly clear: White’s King is completely dominant, while black’s e-pawn has now been labeled as weak. } 3... Re6 4. Rf3+ { [%csl Ge7][%cal Gf6e7] } 4... Ke7 5. g3! { A common“don’t hurry” Rubinstein move. As discussed in the Cat and Mouse section, a player shouldn’t hesitate to make quiet,“fixing moves” if his opponent is helpless. Placing the pawn on g3 gets a pawn off the 2nd rank in case black’s Rook makes its way to d2 after ...Rd6+. } 5... g6 6. Re3 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge3e5,Ge7f6] } 6... Kf6 { [%cal Gc3c4] } 7. c4 { The c-pawn finally becomes a passed monster. Black’s position has, as if by magic, become hopeless! } { [%cal Gb3c4] } 7... bxc4 8. bxc4 Re8 9. Rf3+ Kg5 { Hoping to gain some counterplay by attacking white’s kingside pawns. } 10. c5 e4 11. h4+ Kg4 12. Rf4+ Kxg3 13. Rxe4 Ra8 (13... Rd8+ 14. Kc6 Rc8+ 15. Kb6 { was no picnic either. }) 14. c6 g5 15. hxg5 hxg5 { [%cal Gd5d6,Gd6c7] } 16. c7 Kf3 17. Re1 { 1-0. White will win black’s Rook for the c-pawn, give up his own Rook for black’s g-pawn, then finally have a meal on a6 and promote the a5-pawn. } *


