{ White has an annoying edge. Aside from his superior kingside pawn structure, both his Rook and Knight are more active than their black counterparts. The
main target is the b6-pawn, but before laying siege to it, White fixes black’s kingside pawn weaknesses. } { [%cal Bf6f5] }
1. g4! { A huge move! Now the f6- and h7-pawns are long-term additions to black’s “list of problems.” } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gf3e4,Ge4d3,Gd3c4,Gc4b5,Gb5b6] } 1... h5 { Black can’t wait for White to play Rc6 followed by Ke4-d3-c4-b5 with a total rout. } 2. gxh5 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bf3e4,Be4d3,Bd3c4,Bc4b5] } 2... Kh6 3. Kg4 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gg4h5] } 3... f5+ 4. Kh4 f4 { Trading off one of his potentially weak pawns makes good sense. } 5. Rc8 { [%csl Gh8][%cal Gc8h8] } 5... fxe3 6. fxe3 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge3d4] } 6... Rd5 { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gd5h5] } 7. Rh8+ Kg7 { [%csl Gh8,Ga4] } 8. Ra8 Nc3 { [%csl Ga1][%cal Ga8a7,Ga7f7] } 9. Ra7 { Threatening Nxe6+ but also preparing an attack against black’s King by Nf3 and NgS. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Rd4e6,Gg7f6,Gh5h8] } 9... Re5 { This stops Nxe6+ but gives White time for his kingside dreams. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge5e6] } 10. Nf3 { [%csl Ge3][%cal Ge5e3] } 10... Re4+ (10... Rxe3? 11. Ng5 { f7 can’t be defended. } { [%cal Rg5f7,Ra7f7] }) 11. Kg5 Rxe3? { Black gets overwhelmed after this natural but poor move. Farbetter resistance was offered by 11...Nd5. } (11... Nd5!?) 12. h6+ Kg8 13. h7+ Kxh7 14. Rxf7+ Kg8 { Both sides only have two pawns left, so one might expect Black to have real chances to survive. However, his King is in serious trouble and—by mixing threats to black’s King and pawns—White can eventually pick up the bit on b6 and walk away with a won Rook endgame. } 15. Kg6 Ne4 { Black needs to have his Knight participate in the defense, but the jump to e4 doesn’t get the job done. } (15... Nd5 16. Ng5 e5 (16... Nxb4?? 17. Nh7 { threat of Nf6 mate } { [%cal Rh7f6] }) 17. Ne6 { the threat of Rf8 mate forces black’s reply } { [%cal Rf7f8] } 17... Nf4+ 18. Nxf4 exf4 19. Rxf4 Rh3 20. Kf5 Rxh2 21. Ke6 Rd2 22. Rf5 { with a decisive advantage since White can eventually pick up black’s b-pawn by Rd5, Kd6, b4-b5, and
Ke7xb6. } { [%cal Bb4b5,Be6d6,Gf5d5,Bd6c6,Bc6b6] }) 16. h4 Nd6 17. Rd7 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Ge3f3] } 17... Rxf3 18. Rxd6 { [%csl Gd8][%cal Gd6d8] } 18... Kf8 { [%cal Gh4h5] } 19. Rxe6 Rg3+ (19... Rf4? 20. Rf6+ $18) (19... Rh3 { loses to } 20. Kg5 { or Kg4 also good. }) 20. Kf5 Rg1 (20... Rh3 21. Kg5 Rg3+ 22. Kh5 b5 23. Re5 { with White owning two extra pawns. }) 21. Rxb6 { 1-0 } { [%csl Bb4] } *


