[Event "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): All King and Pawn Endgames are Confusing ! (Martz - Benko, Torremolinos 1973)"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/jymPZaIK/n0SAr9NL"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): All King and Pawn Endgames are Confusing ! (Ma"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/stfeVxpX"]
[FEN "8/pp4pp/2p1K3/2P5/3k3P/1P6/P7/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ Black to move (inversed board)

Black is a pawn up and he has the move, so how hard can this be? Judge for yourself as we follow Benko’s comments from his book, Pal Benko: My Life, Games, and Compositions:

“So we find ourselves in an interesting King and pawn endgame. This kind of ending rarely occurs in modern tournaments, so I was delighted to get the
chance to play such a position.

“Here the game was adjourned and I had to seal my move. A quick calculation convinced me that 41...Kxc5 would be a blunder with a draw..

I must admit that, in the little time I had left, my first plan was 41...Ke4 42.Kf7, would be an easy win.

Also winning for me is 41...Ke4 42.Kd6 Then I noticed that 41...Ke4 42.b4! created some difficulties for me:

Then I noticed that 41...Ke4 42.b4! created some difficulties for me: 42...h5! 43.a4! }
1... g6!! { After considering all of [the other variants], I came to the conclusion that I must find an easier solution. As it happens, there is one in the position. } (1... Ke4 2. b4! (2. Kf7 Kf5 3. Kxg7 h5 $19) (2. Kd6 a5 3. Kc7 Kd5 4. Kb6 g6 5. a3 h6 6. b4 axb4 7. axb4 Kc4 8. Kxb7 Kb5! $19) 2... h5 3. a4! { and now : this position. } 3... Kd4 (3... Kf4 4. b5! g5 5. hxg5 Kxg5 6. b6! (6. a5!? a6!) 6... a5! (6... axb6 7. cxb6 h4 8. a5 { isn’t what Black wants. }) 7. Kd7 h4 8. Kc7 h3 9. Kxb7 h2 10. Ka8 h1=Q 11. b7 Qd5 12. b8=Q Qxc5 { and now White can force a draw with } 13. Qb6! Qc3 14. Kb7 c5 15. Kc6 c4 16. Qb5+ Kf4 17. Kc5 $10) (3... a6! { is full of interesting variations } 4. a5! Kf4 (4... Kd4 5. Kf5 Kc4 6. Kg6 Kxb4 7. Kxg7! Kxc5! 8. Kg6 Kb4 9. Kxh5 c5 10. Kg5 c4 11. h5 c3 12. h6 c2 13. h7 c1=Q+! $19) 5. Kd7 g5 6. hxg5 Kxg5 7. Kc7 h4 8. Kxb7 h3 9. Kxa6 h2 10. Kb7 h1=Q 11. a6 { and a strange position has come : White’s pawn only stands on the 6th rank (which usually means that it’s time to resign) but, despite this, Black has to show a bit of care if wants to reel in the point: } 11... Qb1? (11... Qh7+ { is correct } 12. Kb8 Qg8+ 13. Kb7 Qf7+ 14. Kb8 Qf4+ $19) 12. a7 Qxb4+ 13. Kc7 (13. Kc8?? Qxc5 14. a8=Q Qf8+ 15. Kb7 Qxa8+ 16. Kxa8 c5 $19) (13. Kxc6?? Qe4+ $19 { [%cal Ge4a8] }) 13... Qa5+ 14. Kb7 $10 { and Black doesn’t have time to take the pawn on c5. }) 4. Kd6 (4. Kd7? a5!) 4... Kc4 5. Kc7 Kxb4 6. Kxb7 Kxc5 7. Kxa7 (7. a5? Kd6! 8. Kxa7 Kc7 9. Ka6 g6! $19) 7... Kb4 8. Kb6 Kxa4 9. Kxc6 $10) (1... Kxc5?? 2. Kf7 b5 3. Kxg7 h5 4. Kg6 Kb4 5. Kxh5 c5 6. Kg6 c4 7. bxc4 bxc4 8. h5 $10) 2. Kf6 Ke4! 3. Kg7 Kf4 4. Kh6 Kg4 5. Kxh7 g5! 6. hxg5 Kxg5 7. Kg7 Kf4 8. Kf7 Ke5 9. Ke7 Kd5 10. Kd7 Kxc5 11. Kc7 b5 12. Kb7 a5 13. a3 b4 14. a4 Kd4 15. Kxc6 Kc3 16. Kb5 Kxb3 17. Kxa5 Ka3 { 0-1
I don’t know about you, but I have two thoughts whenever I look at this endgame. One is, “It’s very beautiful.” The other is, “My god! I would never have found any of that over the board!” The message should be clear: never enter a pawn endgame lightly!
Transposition into a pawn ending often marks the final stage of the opponent's demise. However, masters have learned to be very wary of all King and pawn endgames since strange, often unforeseen, complications can turn an easy win into a draw or even loss! } *


