[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ Queen and pawn endgames are notoriously difficult so, in the interest of making his life easier, Black must consider the very forcing 1...Qf3 when it’s clear
that, in the ensuing King and pawn endgame, both sides will eat each other's pawns. After a deep count it might seem that each side will promote with a resulting Queen and pawn vs. Queen endgame. Is that what Black wants, or is something else going on here? }
1... Qf3! { It turns out that this is a dead win! But that decision can only be made if Black notices a subtle point at the end of a long, forcing sequence. } 2. Qxf3 { White doesn’t have much choice } (2. Qh4 Kxb3 $19 { is hopeless, }) (2. Qc7 Qxf2+ $19 { even worse, }) (2. Qg1 Qh3#) 2... gxf3 3. Kg3 Kxb3 4. Kxf3 Kxa4 5. Kg4 Kb5! { Getting out of the way of the a-pawn and simultaneously preparing to stop white’s pawn with the King. } 6. f4 Kc6 7. Kg5 Kd7 8. Kg6 a4 9. f5 a3 10. f6 a2 11. f7 Ke7! { 0-1This was seen by Black when he played 1...Qf3. resigned since 12.Kg7 is met by 12...al=Q+.
Such transitions from “messy” positions to the supposed serenity of a King and pawn endgame can be a blessing ora curse. In the following example White had to decide whether or not to leap into unknown King and pawn waters. } *


[Event "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): All King and Pawn Endgames are Confusing ! (Akiko Uto- Tran, Paris 2005)"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/jymPZaIK/4SBaZjkl"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): All King and Pawn Endgames are Confusing ! (Ak"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/m6qGpy6p"]
[FEN "5k2/5p2/4pNp1/p2bP3/2p2P2/6P1/P2K3P/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ In the game, White avoided the King and pawn endgame and took a walk down the wrong path with 1.Nd7+. The real questions concerning this position are:

- Is the King and pawn endgame worth considering after 1.Nxd5?

- If White retains the Knight, who is better and why?

The King and pawn endgame that arises after 1.Nxd5 exd5 2.g4 proves to be extremely interesting. }
1. Nxd5 exd5 2. g4 { It seems that white’s plan of creating a passed e- and h-pawn (which will be unstoppable due to their distance from each other) will give her the full point: } 2... d4 (2... g5 { A bit of analysis makes it clear that this is a dismal failure } 3. fxg5 Ke7 (3... Kg7 4. h4 Kg6 5. Kc3 Kg7 6. h5 Kh7 7. g6+ fxg6 8. e6 Kg7 9. h6+ $18 { [%cal Rh6h8,Re6e8] }) 4. h4 d4 (4... Ke6 5. h5 Kxe5 6. h6 $18 { [%cal Gh6h8] }) 5. h5 Kf8 6. h6 Kg8 7. g6! fxg6 8. e6 $18) (2... Ke7 { Can Black try ...f7-f6 at some point? Let’s see: } 3. h4 f6 4. h5! { and we have a very pleasing pawn structure that guarantee’s White success: } 4... Kf7 5. e6+! $18) (2... f6! { threatening to win by 3...fxe5 4.fxe5 g5!, fixing two
pawns with one) } { [%cal Rf6e5,Rf4e5,Rg6g5] } 3. e6 (3. exf6 Kf7 4. g5 { This paints a nice visual picture, but it’s only a draw after } 4... d4 5. Ke2 c3 6. Kd3 Ke6 $10 { black’s c- and d-pawns handcuff white’s King, while white’s four kingside pawns are stopped by black’s King and pawn. }) (3. h4! { It seems we might have found a defense for Black, but one last calm look will give us the “answer”: } 3... fxe5 (3... Ke7 4. h5 $18) (3... g5!! { saves the game: } 4. hxg5 (4. exf6?? gxh4 $19) (4. h5 gxf4 (4... fxe5?? 5. fxe5 $18) 5. e6 f3 6. h6 c3+ 7. Kc2! (7. Kxc3 f2 8. h7 f1=Q 9. h8=Q+ Ke7 10. Qc8?? (10. Qg8 { was correct }) 10... Qc1+ { picking up the Queen } { [%cal Gc1c8] }) 7... f2 8. h7 f1=Q 9. h8=Q+ Ke7 10. Qc8! Qe2+ $15 { when only Black has chances }) (4. fxg5 fxe5 { and Black wins IF he can reach the following key
position with White to move (blue arrows): } { [%cal Be5e4,Ba2a4,Bd2d4,Bf8g7,Bg5g6,Bg4g5,Bh4h5] } 5. a4 { (I move to reach that position) } 5... e4 6. Kc3 Kg7 7. h5 Kf7 8. g6+ Kg7 9. g5 Kf8 10. Kd4 Kg7 { Here we are. } (10... Kg8 11. Kc3 Kg7 12. Kd4 { Here is the same position with black to move : } 12... Kg8 13. h6 Kh8 14. g7+ Kh7 15. g6+ Kg8 16. Ke5 e3 17. Kf6 e2 18. h7#) 11. Kc3 e3 12. Kc2 d4 $19 { and black’s pawns are like a tidal wave. }) 4... fxe5 5. fxe5 d4 6. a4 Kf7 7. Kc2 Kg6 8. e6 Kg7 $10) 4. fxe5 Ke7 5. h5 $18 { and White’s back in the win column.
At this point I can see White snapping off black’s Bishop and rushing into this endgame, confident that victory will be hers. However, we’ve missed something! Return to 3.h4... }) 3... Ke7 4. f5 g5 $10 { Are we on to something? Suppose White chops off the pawn after 2...f6 }) 3. h4 (3. f5?? g5 $19 { when the g-pawn stops two white pawns }) 3... Ke7 4. f5 $18 { followed by h5 when Black must resign.
Is it really that simple? Of course not! It’s a pawn endgame and that means there are almost always surprising resources lurking behindthe scenes. So let’s try (after 2.g4) 2...g5. } { [%cal Gh4h5] } *


