[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): ... part 2"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/W0jotTlc"]
[FEN "5k2/5p2/4pNp1/p2bP3/2p2P2/6P1/P2K3P/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ So it turns out that White can’t do more than draw the King and pawn endgame, once again confirming our adage that “all King and pawn endgames are confusing!” Instead, returning to the previous diagram, an experienced player would quickly look over the mess we just explored and then refuse to make the exchange, even if he wasn’t able to plumb the King and pawn endgame’s depths. Why would he make this decision? Because it should be obvious that the minor piece endgame gives White a huge advantage with no risk whatsoever. }
1. Kc3 { White fixes the c-pawn on c4 and ties down black’s Bishoptoits defense. } 1... Kg7 (1... Ke7 { Moving towards the middle doesn’t help: } 2. g4 Kf8 3. h4 Ke7 4. h5 gxh5 5. gxh5 Kf8 6. h6 $18) 2. h4! { This not only gets white’s kingside majority into motion, but also stops all ...g6-g5 based counterplay. } 2... Kh6 3. g4 Kg7 { and now White has many ways to win the game. } 4. a3!? { take her time and play “fixer” moves like this one.
After looking over this endgame, World Championship candidate Yasser Seirawan said: “My thought process would be thatif the King and pawn ending was‘close,’ White should start with 1.Kc3 in order to always get the King and pawn ending she wants by handcuffing the d5-Bishop to the c4-pawn. As Black ‘passes,’ White advances her kingside pawns to g¢ and h4,getting a position where black’s King is on g7 before playing f4-f5. Then she can transpose into the King and pawn ending.” Note that Seirawan made it clear that he would have no interest in entering a “close” position from a position of strength. In other words, why take risks when you can step into the King and pawn endgame on your own (highly favorable) terms?
We've seen players of all strengths having trouble correctly assessing some very complicated King and pawn endgames. However,it’s not uncommon for even world-class players to botch positions that should be well within their range to solve. } (4. Nxd5!? { go into a winning King and pawn endgame } 4... exd5 5. f5 { this is far superior to the King and pawn position we entered earlier—White will follow with h5 and promote one of her pawns) }) (4. g5!? { go right for the win of material } 4... Kf8 5. Nd7+ Ke7 6. Nb6 { when Nxc4 can’t be stopped } { [%cal Gb6c4] }) *


[Event "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): All King and Pawn Endgames are Confusing ! (Najdorf- Mecking, Wijk aan Zee 1978)"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/jymPZaIK/NzhlFnr2"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): All King and Pawn Endgames are Confusing ! (Na"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/x1dHxcqU"]
[FEN "8/p1p4p/8/1p1k4/1P2ppPP/2P2P2/P4K2/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ Black to move (inversed board)

We've seen players of all strengths having trouble correctly assessing some very complicated King and pawn endgames. However,it’s not uncommon for even world-class players to botch positions that should be well within their range to solve.

Our next game, between the legendary Miguel Najdorf and World Championship contender Henrique Mecking,is winning for Black, but he doesn’t come close to winning it! }
1... exf3! (1... e3+?? { In the actual game, Black tossed certain victory away } 2. Ke2 Kc4 3. h5 $10 Kxc3?? 4. g5 $18) 2. Kxf3 Ke5 { wins for the following reasons:
- At some point White will be forced to advance his kingside pawns, whereupon Black will be able to munch on them with his King.
- Trying to hold Black off with King moves (such as Kf2) fails since ...Ke4 followed by ...f3 and ... Kf4 still picks off the kingside pawns.
- Black has two tempo moves on the queenside to white’s one. This is of critical importance! } 3. Kf2 Ke4 4. h5 { we have a “Black to move and win” situation. } (4. g5? Kf5 5. Kf3 a6 6. a3 c6 7. Kf2 Kg4 $19) (4. Ke2? f3+ 5. Kf2 Kf4 6. g5 Kg4 $19) (4. Kf1? Kf3 5. g5 Kg4 $19) 4... Ke5! { I can only guess that Mecking missed this move. Othertries don’t get the job done: } (4... h6?? 5. g5! $18) (4... f3 5. g5 Kf5 6. g6 hxg6 7. hxg6 (7. h6?? Kf6 8. Kxf3 Kf7 9. Kf4 Kg8 10. Kg4 Kh7 11. Kg5 a6 12. a3 c6 $19 { and, thanks to that extra queenside tempo move, White has to drop his h-pawn andwill lose the game) }) 7... Kxg6 8. Kxf3 Kf5 9. Ke3 Ke5 10. Kd3 Kd5 11. Kd2 Kc4 (11... c5 12. Kd3 $10) 12. Kc2 $10) (4... a6 5. Kf1! (5. Ke1 { is the same since } 5... Kf3 6. g5 Kg2 7. g6 hxg6 8. hxg6 f3 9. g7 f2+ 10. Kd2 f1=Q 11. g8=Q+ $10) 5... Ke5 6. Kf2 { and suddenly black’s win is gone since he has tossed away one of his precious queenside tempo moves } 6... Kf6 7. Kf3 Kg5 8. h6! Kxh6 9. Kxf4 $10) 5. Kf3 (5. g5 Kf5 6. g6 hxg6 7. h6 Kf6 8. Kf3 g5 $19) (5. Ke2 Kf6 6. Kf3 Kg5 7. a3 a6 8. h6 c6 $19 { and White loses all his kingside pawns. }) 5... h6! 6. a3 a6 7. Kf2 Kf6 8. Ke2 Kg5 9. Kf3 { Now,after black’s quiet push with his c-pawn, we have a “whoever move’s loses” situation. Thanks to that final queenside tempo move, Black gets the prize. } 9... c6 $19 { andit’s time for Whiteto resign. } *


[Event "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): All King and Pawn Endgames are Confusing ! (Bacrot - Kramnik, Dortmund 2005))"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/jymPZaIK/SBT8C7gD"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): All King and Pawn Endgames are Confusing ! (Ba"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/0WLBWKBp"]
[FEN "8/p2kr2p/P5p1/2R2p2/2P2P2/2P2K2/4P2P/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ White is two pawns up and is clearly winning, yet claiming a theoretical win and actually proving it are often two different things. In the present situation, White has many good moves (1.Rb5 and 1.h4 are good choices), but offering a Rook exchange by 1.Re5 is extremely tempting since, if the resulting King and
pawn endgame is indeed a win, it would avoid the potential complications of a long, drawn out Rook endgame.

Thus we come face to face with a common question: does entry into the King endgame make life easier, or are we setting ourselves up for some strange King
and pawn endgame pitfall? }
1. Re5! { Best, but the two sets of doubled isolated pawns will demand some skill from White before he gains the full point.
UsEFuL ADVICE
If you find yourself facing a decision like this and you have any doubts about the King and pawn endgame that results, avoid it like the plague! } 1... Rxe5 2. fxe5 g5 { Stopping white’s King from penetrating and also gaining counterplay by setting his own pawn majority into motion. } (2... Ke7 { Completely hopeless } 3. Kf4 Ke6 4. h4 (4. c5!? Kd5 5. c4+ Kxc5 6. Kg5 $18) 4... h6 5. c5 Kd5 6. h5 gxh5 7. Kxf5 Kxc5 8. e6 Kd6 9. Kf6 $18) 3. c5 { The material in Part Five should have already taught you that side-by-side passed pawns, separated by one file, guard themselves. For example, 3...Ke6 is met by 4.c6 when 4...Kxe5 5.c7 leads to a new Queen for White. } 3... h6 4. c4 h5 (4... Kd8 { Trying a waiting also fails } 5. Ke3 Ke7 (5... Kc7 6. e6 $18) (5... Kd7 6. Kd4 { intending Kd5 will shortly force Black to lay down his arms: } { [%cal Gd4d5] } (6. c6+ Ke6 7. c5 Ke7 8. Kd4 g4 9. Kd5 f4 10. e6 (10. Ke4!? g3 11. hxg3 fxg3 12. Kf3 $18) 10... f3 11. exf3 gxf3 12. c7 f2 13. c8=Q f1=Q 14. Qd7+ Kf6 15. Qf7+ Kg5 16. Qxf1 $18) 6... Ke6 7. c6 f4 8. c7 Kd7 9. e6+ Kxc7 10. Ke5 Kc6 11. e7 Kd7 12. Kf6 Ke8 13. Ke6 $18 { when the advance of the c-pawn leads to mate in six })) 5. h4 g4+ (5... gxh4 { gets no hope at all } 6. Kg2 $18 { [%cal Gg2h3,Gh3h4] }) 6. Kf4 Ke6 (6... Kd8 7. c6 Kc7 8. e6 Kxc6 9. c5 Kc7 10. e3 Kc6 (10... Kd8 11. c6 $18) 11. Ke5 $18) 7. c6 Ke7 8. c5 Ke6 (8... Kd8?! $18 { forces a new Queen }) 9. c7! { The key to the position. White gives up this pawn so his e-pawn can march down the board with tempo. } 9... Kd7 10. e6+ Kxc7 11. e3! { Most accurate. } 11... Kc6 (11... Kd8 12. c6 { loses immediately, Black must move his King to c6 where it will be in check if the pawn promotes on e8. }) 12. Ke5! { This leads to a winning Queen endgame. He decided to leave that pawn on the board since, in some lines, it would block checks on the f-file by black’s soon-to-be-born Queen. } (12. e7?? Kd7 $19) (12. Kxf5!? $18) 12... g3 13. e7 Kd7 14. Kf6 g2 15. c6+ Kc7 16. e8=Q g1=Q 17. Qd7+ Kb6 18. c7 { 1-0 } 18... Qa1+ 19. Kg6 Qg1+ 20. Kh7 *


