[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ Here’s White can break black’s blockade by Qb5/Qb4, Qc5, and Qc7.

This series of four diagrams has shown us that, in positions with Bishop vs. Bishop, Knight vs. Knight, or Rook vs. Rook, without Kings or other pieces or pawns helping, a firm blockade can’t be broken. However, in a Queen ending, this defensive blockade can easily be torn down by the stronger side’s Queen and pawn.

RULE
Passed pawns have the greatest impact in Queen endgames because a blockade of an advanced passer is very difficult, if not impossible, to maintain.

The following examples should “brutally” push this rule home. } { [%csl Bb4,Bb5,Bc7,Bc5] }
 *


[Event "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (Passed Pawns Rule!) exemple 1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/jymPZaIK/KgIVzw0J"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (Passed Pawns Rule!) e"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/h8t65jPv"]
[FEN "3q4/1Q3ppk/P3p2p/3p4/8/8/8/6K1 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ black to move, draw.

RULE :
In a Queen endgame, a passed pawn's value is dependent on how advanced it is. When both sides have passed pawns, the player with the fastest passer (even if he’s a pawn or two behind) often has a marked advantage.

Though Black is no less than four pawns ahead, white’s single pawn on a6 is so powerful that Black is forced to scramble for the draw (1 ...Qg5+ forces perpetual check). }
1... Qg5+ 2. Kf1 Qf5+ 3. Kg1 Qg6+ 4. Kf1 Qf5+ 5. Kg1 Qg6+ $10 *


[Event "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (Passed Pawns Rule!) exemple 2"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/jymPZaIK/L0jWvXUx"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (Passed Pawns Rule!) e"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/GATUusBq"]
[FEN "1q6/p4pk1/P3p1p1/1P1p3p/8/6P1/5P1K/3Q4 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ White to move and draw

Understanding the rule connected to the previous diagram, allows you to save positions that appear lost, simply because you’ll know that it’s not a material count that matters, but rather the creation and subsequent speed of a passed pawn.

White is two pawns down, apparently for nothing. All of black’s pawns are safe, his King is secure, and white’s queenside majority seems to be going nowhere fast. But, as has already been stated, material often takes a back seat to passed pawns in Queen endgames. Since white’s only plus is his queenside majority, he MUST create a passed pawn in that sector, no matter what the price! }
1. Qd4+ Kg8 (1... Kh7?? 2. Qf4! { when it’s not clear how Black can save the game: } 2... Qb6 (2... Qxf4 3. gxf4 d4 4. Kg1! { intending b6 is an easy win for White, } { [%cal Bb5b6] }) (2... Qe8 3. Qc7 { is also grim for the second player. }) 3. Qxf7+ Kh6 4. Qf8+ Kh7 (4... Kg5? 5. Qf4#) 5. Kg2 d4 6. Qf7+ Kh6 7. Qb7 Qc5 8. b6! $18) 2. b6! { Sacrificing a pawn (which puts him three behind!) in order to create a far advanced passer. } (2. Qc5 { with the idea of Qc6-b7, also adequate } { [%cal Bc5c6,Bc6b7] }) 2... axb6 3. Qa4 Qa7 (3... d4? 4. a7 Qa8 5. Qa6 d3 6. Qxb6 Kh7 7. Qb8 Qf3 8. a8=Q Qxf2+ 9. Qg2 $18 { 1-0 }) 4. Qe8+ Kg7 5. Qc8 { Suddenly it’s clear that white’s a6-pawn—soon to be unblocked by Qb7—is light years ahead of black’s whole armada. } 5... b5 { Realizing that the tide has turned, Black bails out and plays for perpetual check. } 6. Qb7 { The most striking way to force the draw } (6. Kg2 { is a bit less extreme: } 6... Qd4 7. Qb7 Qe4+ 8. Kg1 h4 9. gxh4 Qg4+ $10) 6... Qxf2+ 7. Kh1 Qxg3 { Six pawns to one, yet Black mustplay for the draw! } 8. a7 Qe1+ { 0,5-0,5. Since blocking a far advanced passed pawn is often impossible in a Queen endgame, the defender often resorts to perpetual check as his main “survival mode” weapon. To combat this, there are two basic ways for the stronger side to avoid the perpetual and try for the full point.
- DUEL PURPOSE QUEEN: The Queen escorts the pawn down the board and simultaneously covers the only road available to its King.
- DOUBLED PAWN PROTECTIVE COVER: The doubled pawns around the stronger side’s King actas a wall, blocking any and all checks. } *


[Event "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (Passed Pawns Rule!) Duel Purpose Queen"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/jymPZaIK/njMu3gWx"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (Passed Pawns Rule!) D"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/zBMz36Lo"]
[FEN "2Q5/5p1k/1P4p1/8/7p/7P/4q1P1/7K w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ Let’s look at each of these concepts in action.

RULE :
The Queen has the ability to control the queening square of a passed pawn while simultaneously protecting its King.

White’s monster b-pawn will win the game unless Black can generate some counterplay against white’s King.
Black to move would allow 1...Qd1+ (1...Qe1+ 2.Kh2 Qg3+ 3.Kg1 Qe1+, etc. is an immediate draw) 2.Kh2 Qd6+ followed by 3...Qxb6.
However, the whole “King safety” problem can be taken care of by one simple move. }
1. Qc7 { White’s Queen becomes master of the h2-b8 diagonal (which means that when white’s King moves to h2 it will be immune from harm) while simultaneously forcing through the b-pawn by b7 and b8=Q (the fact that it attacks f7 also doesn’t hurt!). } 1... Qe1+ 2. Kh2 Qa5 { The only chance } 3. Qxf7+ Kh8 (3... Kh6 4. Qf4+ g5 (4... Kh7 5. b7) 5. Qf6+ { with b7 to follow. By staying on the h2-b8 diagonal, White leaves Black no hope of saving the game. } { [%cal Gb6b7] }) 4. Qe8+ Kh7 5. Qe7+ { On e7 the Queen covers the c7- and e5-squares, making it hard to check along the sensitive h2-b8 diagonal. } { [%csl Yc7,Ye5][%cal Ye7c7,Ye7e5] } (5. b7?? Qc7+ { [%cal Gc7b7] }) 5... Kh6 6. Qxh4+ { Though hardly necessary, why not pocket a free pawn and then return to the same position little bit richer? } 6... Kg7 7. Qe7+ Kh6 8. b7 Qa7 9. Qf8+ Kh7 10. b8=Q { 1-0. Note how the new Queen takes up the task of defending the diagonal. } *


[Event "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (Passed Pawns Rule!) Doubled Pawn Protective Cover"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/jymPZaIK/m0NY1iYg"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (Passed Pawns Rule!) D"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/HpDrGn5e"]
[FEN "1Q6/p5pk/5p1p/2P5/8/3q1PP1/5PK1/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ In this position white’s doubled f-pawns form a perfect “anti-check seal” against the attentions of the black Queen. Note that if we took the doubled pawn on f3 and made it “healthy” by placing it on h2, Black would be able to annoy his opponent via checks on d5 and/or e4.

Since the King is safe, White can go about the business of promoting his pawn. }
1. c6 { 1-0 Black can’t bother white’s King or stop the c-pawn’s decisive advance.
Let’s see an illustration of these ideas at the highestlevel.... } *


[Event "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (PP Rule) Rubinstein - Capablanca, St Petersburg 1914"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/jymPZaIK/1a3eQIyR"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Queen and Pawn Endgames (PP Rule) Rubinstein -"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/PkafFSHF"]
[FEN "6k1/1q3pp1/p2Q4/1pp5/7P/2P1P3/P4PP1/6K1 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ Black to move.

Black’s a solid pawn down, his c-pawn is loose, and white’s Queen is more actively placed than black’s. One might think that a superb endgame player like Rubinstein will reel in the full point, but Capablanca is aware of the principles illustrated in this section and knows that he must use his queenside pawn majority to create a passed pawn or face certain defeat. If this calls for the sacrifice of another pawn or two, so be it! }
1... b4! { Immediately striving to make a passed pawn. } 2. c4 { This is white’s best winning try. In the actual game White played 2.Qxc5 } (2. Qxc5 bxc3 3. Qxc3 Qb1+ 4. Kh2 Qxa2 { the powerful passed a-pawn will soon force White to take a draw by perpetual check } 5. Qc5 Kh7 6. Qf5+ g6 7. Qf6 a5 8. g4 a4 9. h5 gxh5 10. Qf5+ Kg7 11. Qg5+ Kh7 12. Qxh5+ Kg7 { the game was agreed drawn. }) (2. cxb4 { leads to an instructive position that beautifully illustrates our “a passed pawnis more important than material” philosophy after : } 2... Qxb4 3. Qxa6 c4 4. Qa8+ Kh7 5. Qe4+ Kg8 6. Qe8+ Kh7 7. Qxf7 c3 $10 { Even though White is ahead by three big pawns, the c-pawni s too strong— White must take a perpetual check. }) 2... Qc8! { twofold idea: Black intends both 3...Qf5 with ...Qb1 and ...Qxa2 to follow, and also 3...a5 followed by 4...a4 when a passed pawn will soon be born. } { [%cal Yf5b1,Yb1a2,Bc8f5,Ba6a5,Ba5a4] } (2... a5?? 3. Qd8+ Kh7 4. Qxa5 $16) (2... b3?? 3. axb3 Qxb3 4. Qxa6 $18) (2... Qe4? { Tempting but inaccurate } 3. Qxc5 Qb1+ 4. Kh2 Qxa2 5. Qxb4 Qxf2 6. Qe7! $18) (2... Qa7 { apparently, should (along with 2...Qc8) prove adequate: } 3. g4 (3. Qd8+ Kh7 4. Qa5 Qe7 5. Qxa6 Qxh4 6. Qb5 Qe7 { is okay, but I prefer 5...Qe4, =. I’ll let you figure out the variations here! }) 3... a5 4. h5 a4 { and, as always, the passed pawn will prove to be equivalent to a small nuke: } 5. g5 b3 6. g6 Qa8 7. axb3 a3 8. Qd7?? (8. gxf7+ Kxf7 9. Qg6+ Kg8 10. h6 Qa7 11. h7+ Kh8 12. Qe8+ Kxh7 13. Qh5+ $10 { draw by perpetual check and would be the sane way to play for White }) 8... a2 9. gxf7+ Kf8 $19) 3. Qb6 { This freezes black’s a-pawn and, apparently, ties black’s Queen downto the defense of both a6 and cS. } 3... Qf5! { Refusing to buy into the “defend your pawns passively” myth. } 4. Qxa6 Kh7! { It’s interesting how Black, now two pawns down, can play a quiet move like this and still assure himself of good chances. The idea behind 4...Kh7 is clear: he wants to avoid an unpleasant back rank check after the Queen eventually penetrates to bi or c2. } 5. Qa7 (5. Qb7 { As usual, allowing Black to create an advanced passed pawnis a bad idea: } 5... Qb1+ 6. Kh2 Qxa2 7. Qxf7 b3 8. Qh5+ Kg8 9. Qd5+ Kh7 10. Qxc5 b2 { and White should bail out with perpetual check. }) 5... f6! { Another calm move, telling White that he has no good way to improve his position. } (5... Qe5!? { Also possible with the usual idea of giving up lots of pawns to create an advanced passed b-pawn. }) 6. Qe7 (6. Qa4 Qb1+ 7. Kh2 Qb2 { Also fine for Black with annoying threats }) (6. e4 { The most dangerous move when } 6... Qxe4?? (6... Qe5! 7. Qf7 Qa1+ 8. Kh2 Qxa2 { gives Black enough counterplay to draw. }) 7. Qxc5 $18) 6... Qb1+ 7. Kh2 Qxa2 8. Qe4+ Kh8 9. Qe8+ Kh7 10. Qh5+ Kg8 11. Qxc5 b3 { when, as we’ve seen time and again, the strength of the passed pawn, mixed with a Queen’s ability to torment the enemyKing,should allow Black an easy draw. (Some of the variations were culled from those by Paul Keresin the classic The Art of the Middle Game.) } *


