[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR MASTERS (2200-2399): Entombed and Dominated Minor Pieces (Eddy- Sil"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/qCE9dN32"]
[FEN "2r5/pp2k3/4b1p1/5p1p/4p3/P3P1PB/1P1RPK1P/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "07:00:25"]

{ Black to move.

This position looks like it should be a fairly simple win for Black since he’s in effect a Bishop ahead. Due to this, black’s only real question is whether or not he can penetrate into the queenside with his King after the exchange of Rooks. If he can (while also retaining a queenside pawn), it’s gameover. If he can’t, it might be a draw.

Look at the next three typical positions that Black will be sure to avoid.

In the first two illustrative diagrams (Ch. 44 and 45), black’s King can’t enter the white position and the game is drawn. In the third diagram (46), black’s King has managed to penetrate but all the queenside pawns are gone. This too, is a draw.

With these failures in mind, black’s play is easy to understand: he will exchange Rooks when his King can penetrate into the queenside, and he will be sure to retain a queenside pawn. }
1... a5 { This gains space and also showsthat Black is in no hurry. White is completely helpless so Black should only begin the decisive phase (i.e., trading the Rooks) after he’s optimized his King and pawns. } 2. Ke1 Bb3 3. Kf2 Rc1 4. Bg2 g5 5. h3 g4 6. h4 b5 7. Bf1 Ke6? { Failing to embrace a true Cat and Mouse mentality. This well-intentioned but lazy (and hasty!) move allows White a bit of counterplay by Rd8. Though the position after 8.Rd8 would still be quite lost, why give the “helpless” opponent anything at all? } (7... Rc2?? 8. Rxc2 Bxc2 9. b4 axb4 10. axb4 Ke6 11. Ke1 Kd5 12. Kd2 $10 { when White achieves one of our drawing illustrative diagrams. }) (7... Rc8! { (taking the d8-square away from white’s Rook) } 8. Bg2 { Ah, the sweet sight of the helpless opponent doing absolutely nothing! } 8... Ke6 9. Bf1 Rc2 10. Ke1 (10. Rd8 { is now hopeless thanks to : } 10... Rxb2) 10... Rxd2 11. Kxd2 b4 $19 { when we end up back in our actual game. }) 8. Bg2 { White should have played the last gasp 8.Rd8 and hoped for the best. } 8... Rc2! { Black can now exchange Rooks in optimal fashion } 9. Ke1 (9. Rxc2 { other choice } 9... Bxc2 10. b4 axb4 11. axb4 Kd5 12. Kf1 Kc4 $19 { and black’s King picks up white’s b-pawn. } { [%cal Gc4b4] }) (9. Rd8 { other choice } 9... Rxb2 10. Rh8 Bc4 11. Bf1 Ra2 12. Rxh5 Rxa3 $19) 9... Rxd2 10. Kxd2 b4! { The Bishop on b3 and pawn on b4 form a wall against white’s King. This allows the black King to calmly march to a4 or c4 and enter the white position. } { [%csl Yc2,Yc3,Yc4][%cal Yb3c2,Yb3c4,Yb4c3,Be6c4,Be6c6,Bc6a4] } 11. axb4 axb4 12. Bh1 Kd5 13. Bg2 Kc4 14. Bf1 Ba4 15. Kc1 Kb3 16. Kb1 Bb5 { Forcing White to make a highly unpleasant decision: give up e2 or let black’s King into a2 or c2. } 17. Kc1 Ka2 18. Kc2 Ba4+ 19. Kc1 Bd1! { There were many ways to win, but this is the most fun. It graphically shows just how bad the entombed Bishop really is. } 20. Bg2 (20. Kxd1 Kxb2 $19 { would be powerless to stop the promotion of the b-pawn. } { [%cal Gb4b1,Gb4b3,Gb3b2,Gb2b1] }) 20... Bxe2 21. Kc2 Bd3+ 22. Kc1 b3 23. Bh1 Bf1 { White’s poor Bishop was first entombed, and now dominated. } *


