[Event "Lasker - Marshall World Championship Match"]
[Site "USA"]
[Date "1907.01.26"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Frank James Marshall"]
[Black "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C65"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Berlin Defense, Nyholm Attack"]
[StudyName "Endgame (2200-2399)"]
[ChapterName "Frank James Marshall - Emanuel Lasker"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/iUmzy9iJ/MqhC1etG"]

{ Dr. Emanuel Lasker (1868-1941), the legendary German World Champion who held the title from 1894-1921, had a rather unusual style. His openings were poor, and his middlegame play, while powerful, didn’t have the fluid feel of Rubinstein’s. His strengths, though, combined to create a player that dwarfed the world’s best during his prime years, andinstilled fear into the hearts of his competitors long after he gave up the crown to Capablanca. These strengths were: An untiring fighting spirit, endless optimism, a magnificent tactical vision, unsurpassed defensive skills, and an almost flawless command of the endgame. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d4 exd4 5. O-O Be7 6. e5 Ne4 7. Nxd4 O-O 8. Nf5 d5 9. Bxc6 bxc6 10. Nxe7+ Qxe7 11. Re1 Qh4 12. Be3 f6 13. f3 fxe5 14. fxe4 d4 15. g3 Qf6 16. Bxd4 exd4 17. Rf1 Qxf1+ 18. Qxf1 Rxf1+ 19. Kxf1 { Here we start. a position that the American attacking genius probably expected to draw. Somehow, Lasker made it look easy.
Capablanca said, “In this position it is black’s move. ‘To a beginne rthe position may look like a draw, but the advanced player will realize immediately that there are great possibilities for Black to win, not only because he has the initiative, but because of white’s underdeveloped queenside and the fact that a Bishop in such a position is better than a Knight. It will take some time for White to bring his Rook and Knightintothe fray, and black can utilize it to obtain an advantage. There are two courses open to him. The most evident, and the one that most players wouldtake, is to advance the pawn to c5 and c4 immediately in conjunction with the Bishop check at a6 and any other move that might be necessary with the black Rook. The other, more subtle, course was taken by Black.” } 19... Rb8 20. b3 Rb5! { The Rook is very active on the 5th rank, and can torment white’s pawns by moving to a5, c5, e5 or even h5. } 21. c4 { This makes sense because it avoids attacks against c2 and also closes down the a6-f1 diagonal (which black’s Bishop might well have madeuse of). However, the resulting positions turn out to be remarkably difficult for White to deal with. } (21. Nd2 Rc5 { Defenses like that were all possible (and might well have been better than what was actually played), but they look risky and one can understand why Marshall avoided them. } 22. Nc4 (22. c4 dxc3 23. Nf3) (22. Nf3 Rxc2 23. Nxd4) 22... Ba6 23. Ke2 Bxc4+ 24. bxc4 Rxc4 25. Kd3 Rc3+ 26. Kxd4 Rxc2) 21... Rh5 22. Kg1 c5 23. Nd2 Kf7 { Black’s plan is now clear: he wants to play ...Bg4 (dominating the Knight) and then bring his King up to eS. After that, ...d4-d3 followed by ...Kd4 is very strong. } { [%cal Bf7e5,Bd4d3,Be5d4,Bc8g4] } 24. Rf1+? { A mistake that forces Black to continue with his plan. He should have tried for queenside counterplay with a3 followed by b4. } 24... Ke7 25. a3 Rh6! { A fine move that renews the Rook’s flexibility by leaving the 5th rank for the wide open 6th. Now ...Ra6 looms on the horizon. } 26. h4 (26. b4 Ra6 27. Rf3 Bg4 28. Rb3 Bd1 29. Rb1 Bc2 30. Rc1 d3 $15) 26... Ra6 { Probing at the weakness on a3. If he can get White to play a3-a4, then ideas of freeing himself with b3-c4 would be a thing of the past. } 27. Ra1 Bg4 28. Kf2 Ke6 { A nice picture: Black’s King is better than white’s, his Rook is far superior to the thing on a1, and the Bishop is dominating the Knight. } 29. a4 (29. Nf3 { doesn’t help: } 29... Bxf3 30. Kxf3 Ke5 $19 { the threat of ...Rf6+ gives Black a winning game. } { [%cal Ra6f6] }) 29... Ke5 30. Kg2 Rf6 { White is completely helpless } 31. Re1 d3 32. Rf1 Kd4 { Black’s plan is a major success. I’m sure Marshall was wondering how he had allowed such a horrible position to occur! } 33. Rxf6 gxf6 34. Kf2 c6 35. a5 a6 { The first zugzwang. Any pawn move hangs something, a Knight move gives up e4, and Kel allows the decisive ...Kc3 when White would be forced into heavy material losses. } 36. Nb1 Kxe4 37. Ke1 Be2 38. Nd2+ Ke3 39. Nb1 f5 { Taking his time and not allowing White any counterchances. White is helpless, so why not make everything perfect before beginning the final phase? } 40. Nd2 h5 { Another zugzwang. White must step back to b1. } 41. Nb1 Kf3 42. Nc3 Kxg3 43. Na4 f4 44. Nxc5 f3 45. Ne4+ Kf4 46. Nd6 { The last several moves were all forced. Now both 28...Ke3 and 28...Kg3 (threatening ...f2+) would be parried by 29.Ne4+. } 46... c5! { Onefinal zugzwang! 29.Kd2 f2 and 29.Kf2 d2 both lose immediately, while 29.Nb7 Kd3 leads to mate. That only leaves the suicidal advance of the b-pawn. } 47. b4 cxb4 48. c5 b3 49. Nc4 Kg3 50. Ne3 b2 { his game was from a fifteen game match they played in New York. Though Marshall was considered to be one of the world’s best players in 1907, Lasker mercilessly routed him 8 wins to none, with 7 draws. Though he excelled in all kinds of endgames(as we’ve just seen!), his ability to save poor or even lost endgamepositions has never been equaled through the span of chess history. } { 0-1 White resigns. } 0-1


