1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 Na5 9. Bc2 c5 10. d3 O-O 11. Nbd2 Re8 12. Nf1 Qc7 13. Bg5 Rb8 14. a3 Be6 15. h3 h6 16. Bd2 Nc6 17. Qe2 Bf8 18. a4 b4 19. c4 Nd7 20. Be3 g6 21. N3d2 f5 22. exf5 gxf5 23. Qh5 Nf6 24. Qg6+ Qg7 25. Qxg7+ Bxg7 26. f4 Nd7 27. Rad1 Bf7 28. Nb3 exf4 29. Bxf4 Nd4 30. Nxd4 Bxd4+ 31. Kh2 b3 { Here we start.
Lasker’s position is completely lost! Let’s see what Reuben Fine had tosay: “White’s light-squared Bishop is stalemated, his pawn structure hopelessly weak, his other pieces (with the exception of the dark-squared Bishop) have little or no freedom. In such positions, Lasker’s conception of chess as a struggle has valuable practical applications: Lasker makes the win as difficult as possible for his opponent, and he seizes upon any slight chance which his opponent may regard as unimportant.” } 32. Rxe8+! { An important move that cuts down on black’s options since now he must take back with his Bishop } (32. Bb1 Bh5! (32... Ne5 33. Ng3 { gives White undeserved play }) 33. g4 fxg4 34. Rxe8+ Rxe8 35. Ng3 Bg6 36. hxg4 Nb6 37. a5 Na4 $19 { with a dead win. }) 32... Bxe8 (32... Rxe8?? 33. Bxb3 { is something White can only dream of!) }) 33. Bb1 Ne5 { The entombed Bishop on b1 makes it seem that the position should be won by itself. Thus, it’s understandable that Black didn’t want to let white’s pieces make noise after ...Bxb2 } (33... Bxb2 34. Ne3 Bg6 35. Bxd6 Re8 36. Nd5 { though } 36... Bd4 { -centralizing and stopping any d3-d4 nonsense—leaves White in a bad way.
his hesitation to “wage war from a position of strength” ultimately comes back to haunt Black. }) 34. Ng3 Bg6? { Note how Dus Chotimirski became lazy due to his crushing position, while Lasker’s play forced his opponent to either wake up and play well or lose much of his advantage. } (34... Bd7 35. Rd2 Nf7 36. a5 Re8 37. Ne2 Bf6 { [%cal Gf6d8,Gd8a5] } 38. d4 $15 { when his position is far better than it initially was. }) (34... Bxb2! { Instead of all this delicacy on black’s part, it was time for him to cash in with, when } 35. Nxf5 Bxa4 { leads to complicated play (just what Black was trying to avoid!), } 36. Nxh6+ (36. Nxd6 Rf8 { should ultimately allow Black to prevail }) 36... Kh8 { is hopeless for White }) 35. Rd2 Rb4?! (35... Nf7 { He still could have retained an advantage guarding d6 and h6, and also allowing the dark-squared Bishop to retreat if it’s attacked by white’s Knight. }) 36. Ne2 Rxa4? (36... Bf2 { Keeping the dark-squared Bishop still gave Black some chances. }) 37. Nxd4 cxd4 38. c5! { Suddenly White is getting real counterplay. } 38... Ra1? { Like a wounded animal, I would guess that Black couldn’t understand what was happening to him. After the move played, White actually emerges with the superior game. } (38... Bh5 { could have been tried, threatening ...Ra1 since White can’t guard his Bishop with Rd1 anymore) } { [%cal Ra4a1] } 39. cxd6 Nd7 { but : } 40. Rf2! { intending Rf1 seems fine. } { [%cal Gf2f1] }) (38... Be8! { Black’s last chance to keep some pressure } 39. cxd6 Nf7 40. Rd1 Bb5 { keeping ...Ra1 in reserve. } (40... Ra1? 41. Bc2!)) 39. cxd6 Nd7 40. Rd1 { Threatening Bc2! } { [%cal Rb1c2] } 40... Bf7 41. Re1 { Lasker’s choice forces Black to deal with some straight forward threats—not an easy thing for Dus Chotimirski to face since his confidence waslikely smashed to jelly by this time. 10.Re1 is also safe since, in case of perfect Black play, White can always force a draw. } (41. Bxh6 { Also possible but that lets Black gain a tempo for queenside counterplay by : } 41... a5 42. Re1 a4 43. Bg5 { One fun possibility } 43... a3 44. bxa3 Rxa3 { v } (44... b2? 45. Bh4) 45. Re7 Nf8 46. Rb7 Ra1 47. Rb8 Be6 48. Bh6 Rxb1 49. Rxf8+ Kh7 50. Re8 b2 51. Rxe6 Rh1+ 52. Kxh1 b1=Q+ 53. Kh2 Qb3 54. Rf6 (54. d7!? Qxe6 55. d8=Q Qe5+ { with a draw by perpetual check }) 54... Qxd3 55. d7 Qb5 56. d8=Q Qe5+ { with a draw by perpetual check. }) 41... a5 42. Re7 Nc5?? { Black cracks and manages to complete his nightmare by losing a position that was once won. } (42... Nf8 { could have been played when : } 43. Re1 Nd7 44. Re7 Nf8 $10 { would be drawn. }) 43. Be5! Ra4 (43... Rxb1 44. Bxd4 Ne6 (44... Rc1 45. Bxc5 Rxc5 46. d7 $18) 45. d7 Nd8 46. Re8+ $18) 44. Rc7 Ne6 45. d7 Rb4 46. Rc8+ Kh7 47. Rh8+ Kg6 48. Re8 { Lasker’s technique is brutal. He wants to get every last drop of juicy goodness from the position. } 48... Rb6 49. Bf4! (49. Rxe6+ Rxe6 50. d8=Q Rxe5 $18 { Not settling for }) 49... Nxf4 50. d8=Q Rc6 51. Re7 { Poor Dus Chotimirski must have been emotionally devastated after this loss. } { 1-0 Black resigns. } 1-0


