1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 12. Nbd2 cxd4 13. cxd4 Bd7 14. Nf1 Rfc8 15. Re2 Nh5 16. dxe5 dxe5 17. Nxe5 Bxh3 18. Nxf7 Be6 19. Ng5 Bc4 20. Bd3 Rd8 21. Rc2 Nf4 22. Bxf4 Qxf4 23. Nh3 Qe5 24. Bxc4+ Nxc4 25. Qe2 Rd4 26. f3 Rad8 27. Rac1 Bc5 28. Kh1 Bb4 29. b3 Nd2 30. Ne3 Ba3 31. Rd1 Bb4 32. a3 Ba5 33. b4 Bc7 34. f4 Nxe4 35. Kh2 Rxd1 36. Nxd1 Qe7 37. Rxc7 Qxc7 38. Qxe4 Qc4 39. Qe7 Qc8 40. Ndf2 h6 41. Qa7 Qe6 42. Qb7 Qd5 43. Qb6 Rd6 44. Qe3 Re6 45. Qc3 Qc4 46. Qf3 Qc6 47. Qd3 Rd6 48. Qb3+ Qd5 49. Qb1 Re6 50. Ng4 Re2 51. Nxh6+ gxh6 52. Qg6+ Kf8 53. Qxh6+ Ke8 54. Qg6+ Kd8 55. Qg3 Re8 56. Qf2 Rg8 57. Qb2 Qd6 58. Qc3 Kd7 59. Qf3 Kc7 60. Qe4 Rg7 61. Qf5 Re7 62. Ng5 Re3 63. Ne4 Qe7 64. Nf6 Kb8 65. g3 Rxa3 66. Kh3 Ra1 67. Nd5 Rh1+ 68. Kg2 Qh7 69. Qxh7 Rxh7 70. Kf3 Kb7 71. g4 Kc6 72. Ke4 Rh8 { Visually White seems to be doing well—his two connected passed pawns seem very dangerous. The reality of the situation, though, is quite different. White’s kingside pawns are far slower than one might suppose due to the Rook’s defensive powers, while ...a6-a5 will create a passed pawn for Black, which the Knight will have serious trouble dealing with.
The immediate threat is 1...Re8+ 2.Kd4 Rd8, winning the * pinned Knight. Thus, White moves it to safety. } { [%cal Rh8e8,Re4d4,Re8d8] } 73. Ne3 Re8+ 74. Kd4 Rd8+ 75. Ke4!? { This keeps the King close to his kingside pawns. However, the published analysis of 3.Kc3!? doesn’t seem particularly compelling. } (75. Kc3!? { Edward Lasker said he intended : } 75... a5 (75... Rd6! { In My Great Predecessors, Part I, Kasparov or Plisetsky simply say Rd6! is unpleasant. The position after : } 76. g5 Kd7 77. f5 Ke8! { (this King move avoids lots of unpleasant checks in many variations) is a very interesting one. To say the position is “unpleasant”. is a bit funny, since the whole endgame is unpleasant for White, whether he plays 3.Ke4 or 3.Kc3. The real question is, can Black win by force after 3.Kc3? Theidea of 3...Rd6 is clear: to allow White to advance his pawns so they become vulnerable to attacks by black’s Rook and King (The King will swing over to the kingside via ...Kd7, ...Ke7, etc.).
To make matters worse, Knight moves will allow Black to target b4 by ...Rc6+ followed by ...Re4. Naturally, 3...Rd6 also allows black’s King run to the kingside by stepping behind the Rook, thus keeping the d-file a no-fly zone
for white’s King. } 78. Ng4 { One illustration of the problems White } (78. Kc2 Rd4 79. Kc3 Rf4 { really IS extremely unpleasant! }) 78... Kf8 79. Ne3 Rd8 { when White is in a mini-zugzwang since a Knight move allows ...Rc8+ followed by ...Rc4 while a King movelets the Rook into d4. Nevertheless, can even these facts guarantee victory? One important line: } 80. Ng2 Rc8+ 81. Kd4! Rc4+ 82. Ke5 Rxb4 83. Nf4 { and I don’t believe Black can win. } 83... Rb1 { (For exemple) } 84. Ne6+ Kg8 85. f6 Rg1 86. Kf5 b4 87. g6 Rf1+ 88. Ke5 b3 89. f7+ Rxf7 90. gxf7+ Kxf7 91. Ng5+ Ke7 92. Ne4 b2 93. Nd2 a5 94. Kd4 { and White successfully stops the pawns.
I'll leave a truly in-depth analysis of the position after 3...Rd6 to someone more inclined to spending weeks fishing for its secrets. After all, this section of the book is titled Endgames for Pure Pleasure, and not The Pain of Drowning in Chess Variations. }) 76. bxa5 Kc5 77. g5 b4+ 78. Kc2 b3+ 79. Kb2 Rd2+ { This analysis contains a couple flaws. The first blip is, instead of 5.g5, 5.Nc2, when I don’t see how Black can win. However, even after 5.g5 b4+ 6.Kc2 b3+, White should avoid the blunder 7.Kb2?? and instead give 7.Kc3 a try when, once again, I’m not sure Black can force a win. }) 75... a5 { With white’s King away from the queenside, the passed b-pawn that this move creates will cost White a high price to stop. } 76. bxa5 b4 77. a6! { Forcing Black to deal with the a~pawn and thus gaining enough time to set up his upcoming defensive scheme. } (77. g5? b3 78. Nc4 Kc5 79. Nb2 Rd2 80. Nd3+ Kc4 81. Ne5+ Kc3 { Alekhine correctly pointed out that wins for Black. }) 77... Kc5 (77... b3 78. Nc4 Kb5 79. Nb2 Kxa6 80. Ke3 Kb5 81. g5 Kb4 82. g6 Kc3 83. Na4+ Kc2 84. f5 Re8+ 85. Kf3 Re5 (85... Rf8 86. g7 Rxf5+ 87. Kg4 Rf1 88. Kg5 $10) 86. Kf4 Ra5 87. Nb2! Kxb2 88. g7 Ra4+ 89. Kg3 Ra8 90. f6 Kc3 91. f7 b2 $10) 78. a7! b3!? (78... Ra8 { It’s not clear if this allows a draw or not, so perhaps the Fritz suggestion of (mentioned in My Great Predecessors, Part I) was the way to go. This whole endgame is so complicated that many of the positions have yet to be analyzed to definite conclusions. }) 79. Nd1 Ra8 80. g5 Rxa7 81. g6 Rd7 (81... Kd6 { My Great Predecessors, Part I once again bows to computer analysis, which suggests that Kd6 wins. } 82. Kf5 (82. Kd4! Rc7 83. g7! Rxg7 84. Kc4 { sets up the same defensive formation that occurred in the actual game. }) 82... Ke7 { and show that things look grim for White. but no }) 82. Nb2 Rd2 83. Kf3! Rd8 (83... Rxb2 84. g7) 84. Ke4 Rd2 85. Kf3 Rd8 86. Ke4 Kd6 87. Kd4! { Rushing to the queenside so the King can deal with the black pawn, but sacrificing his own remaining pawns to do so. } 87... Rc8 88. g7 Ke6! 89. g8=Q+ Rxg8 90. Kc4 Rg3 { Black must hold onto his b-pawn since most Rook vs. Knight endgames (with no pawns) are easily drawn. Edward Lasker (in his classic little book, Chess for Fun and Chess for Blood) wrote: “This is the move I had calculated would win my game after all. The other contestants also believed I had now fairly easy win as White could not capture my pawn. I rememberI left the room at this stage to stretcha little and was congratulated upon my victory by Bogoljuboff and others who were in the pressroom and told me the story of the gamewasreadyto be released. However, when I returned to the table, a rude shock awaited me.” } (90... Rb8 { The only other move worth consideration. Shereshevsky (from
his excellent book, Endgame Strategy) gave the following explanation as to why the game is drawn: “After 16...Rb8 Black again loses his pawn: } 91. Kc3 Kf5 92. Nd3 Rb6 { Now : } 93. Nb2 (93. Kb2 { bad due to : } 93... Ke4) 93... Kxf4 94. Na4 Rb8 95. Nb2 { Black has no possibility of winning. As soon as the black King reaches e1 with the white King at c3 and Knight at b2, there immediately follows Na4 ...Kd1 Kb2, forcing the win of the pawn by Nc5.” }) 91. Na4 Kf5 92. Kb4 Kxf4 93. Nb2! { Edward Lasker: “I was certainly surprised when I saw this move. Examining the position carefully, I soon realized that I had no wayof driving white’s King away. And I could not cross the 6th rank without exposing the pawn to capture! The first thing I did was to rush back to the pressroom and tell the reporters that they should kill their story. I was afraid they might have already released it, for everyone had been telling them I had an easy win. Then I returned for another analysis of the position. If I could reach d2 with the King by playing him in back of my Rook, I could still win. And I made a last attempt.” } 93... Ke4 94. Na4 Kd4 95. Nb2 Rf3 96. Na4 Re3 97. Nb2 Ke4 98. Na4 Kf3 99. Ka3! { Edward Lasker: “This foils my plan. After ...Ke2 white would play Kb2 and I could never approach.” } 99... Ke4 (99... Ke2 100. Kb2 { could never approach. }) 100. Kb4 Kd4 101. Nb2 Rh3 102. Na4 Kd3 103. Kxb3 Kd4+ { This game took over thirteen hours to complete! } { 1/2-1/2 The game is a draw. } 1/2-1/2


